> Per 1{,}000 decisions at 768 tokens, against the calculator's choices, pay-your-bid pricing on the empirical curve moves \$18k--\$83k of expected revenue to sellers and costs bidders \$15k--\$36k of surplus, mostly on Cartier; on the order-statistic curve the figures are \$16k--\$62k and \$5k--\$14k. The tilt toward high bids is a fact about the menu, but whether it is overbidding depends on the curve: 81--91\% of the wrong answers of DeepSeek-8B and the Qwen models bid above the calculator on the empirical curve (55--61\% by chance), but only 38--57\% of all models' wrong answers bid above the optimum on the order-statistic curve and 47--66\% on the first-price equilibrium curve. Under eBay's own rule, where the winner pays about the closing price, higher bids are weakly better, as theory predicts: the same choices raise both seller revenue (\$5k--\$26k) and bidder surplus (\$8k--\$21k for five models), and five models' budget-zero answers score above the calculator's choice (0.91--0.98 vs 0.89). So the cost of the errors is a property of pay-your-bid pricing, and the dollar figures here are pay-your-bid figures.
