> Taking the option with the largest value the trace wrote, once two values exist, recovers 73--96\% of the gap at 768 tokens and 65--88\% at 512 (intervals within $\pm$6 points), where the answer recovers 17--56\%; it never lowers $q$ by more than 0.026 at any budget and changes by at most 4 points for segment windows of 300--1{,}000 characters (Table~\ref{tab:main}, Figure~\ref{fig:main}c). These eBay figures are in-sample. With its settings chosen on Palm alone, the text reader recovers 71--91\% on Cartier and Xbox (answer 29--80\%), but on the synthetic traces it lowers recovery by 3--14 points for four models, so it does not transfer as a replacement for the answer. For Granite-8B it also copies the 14\% of values the model miscomputes. As an audit it does better: sending decisions where reader and answer disagree to the calculator recovers 84--100\% of the gap on eBay (in-sample), 73--93\% on Cartier and Xbox with the Palm-chosen rule, and 82--100\% on synthetic traces, calling the calculator in 13--57\% of decisions. At the same call rate it beats calling on low letter probability, a random call and, except for Granite-8B per call, calling whenever the list is unfinished (e.g.\ Phi-mini 97\% vs 87\%, 82\% and 56\%). Calling it every time recovers everything, so the audit is useful only where reasoning must stay in the loop, for example to check an agent whose tools cannot be changed.
